<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Don't get me wrong, I absolutely love the fever dream imagery that you guys used here, especially in the exploration logs and in the description of the monoliths. I loved the amount of creativity that was put into this, and I really liked the idea of describing weird dream space and movement through it in terms of dimensions (4th dimension, 5th dimension, etc.). I really liked the strangely tragic ending of too. Plus, as stated by other posters, this article captured the mystery aspect of the contest super well. I can tell you, right now, that this is one of those articles whose images and story stuck with me days after I've read it.</p>
<p>However, maybe I'm not understanding it, but I just think that the core anomaly is logically inconsistent (yeah, I know, ironic). The article states that SCP-5000 can spread through different concepts and that any sentient being/sufficiently advanced AI can accidentally stumble upon it; which seems to be a possibility, given that &quot;partial assimilation of sections of the Noosphere pertaining to visual abstraction, conjecture, and metacognition&quot; has occurred, yet at the same time, it's self-contained and classified as Safe.</p>
<p>The first sentence of the description kind of throws me for a loop too. The more I try to analyze it, the more I find that I don't really understand what it really means in the context of what exactly the anomaly is (even though the Foundation appears to have a pretty good understanding of it).</p>
<p>It's also apparently &quot;biologically self-contained within an organically inaccessible region of the Noosphere,&quot; but at the same time, the article says that the Monoliths can breach themselves into physical reality if they grow big enough, allowing nearby sentient beings to get sucked into the Noosphere.</p>
<p>As I understand it, even thinking of or being conceptually associated with the Anchor would put you at risk of being affected, too, as described in the line, &quot;SCP-5000's influence is capable of spreading through physical, mental, or conceptual proximity to the Anchor&quot;.</p>
<p>With everything considered, it looks like the anomaly would easily be an unstoppable Keter, or would even have lead to some kind of in-progress K-class scenario, especially since some of the Monoliths include enormous clock faces at each of the Earth's poles and a giant metal sphere that's half the size of the moon.</p>
<p>Let me know if there's any misconceptions that I have that you guys can clear up, since I do like this article a lot.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

