					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/natvoltaic" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2800867); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2800867&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608238035" alt="NatVoltaic" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2800867)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/natvoltaic" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2800867); return false;" >NatVoltaic</a></span> <span class="odate time_1585025028 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Mar 2020 04:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4562129">
									

<p>Technobabble requires a very specific finesse to pull off &#8212; the ability to juggle complex concepts, to reduce them to terms whose meaning can still be inferred or quickly explained, and to apply them in ways which are understandable in the context of the story. Am I going to claim I know how to achieve this perfectly? No. I've fucked up plenty of times, and for as long as I'm practicing I'll be fucking up in different ways.</p>
<p>However, there still comes a point when I'm reading something where, if it becomes so dense I lose track of what it is I'm supposed to be focusing on, I am going to be completely lost in the story. Not because I'm necessarily confused &#8212; I was able to follow along with the meaning here &#8212; but because I'm lost on what it is I'm supposed to <em>care</em> about. The technobabble here is layered so thick and so without relent that I don't know why I should care about any of what's happening. I tried to search for it, but by the halfway point I lost my patience.</p>
<p>I get that the intent here is for the mystery to be in deciphering what the terminology all means. That isn't something I inherently dislike. SCP-2719, one of my favorite articles, has an understanding which hinges on five words of technobabble. However, the difference between this and 2719 is that I could read 2719 without knowing the meaning and still be viscerally <em>horrified</em>. The surreal, apparent logic and the small details instill a reaction of &quot;what the fuck&quot; which is independent of knowing what the technobabble means. Said reaction is what also made me incessantly search for any explanation of what it is I had read. Not because of the terminology on its own, but because of everything else it supplemented &#8212; everything which made me <em>care</em> about what the terminology meant.</p>
<p>This article is the technobabble and the technobabble alone. I couldn't find a single reason for me to decipher a meaning through all of it.</p>
<p>-1.</p>

